### What is unsafe publication?

Without safe publication, the JVM may reorder the constructor's field assignments and the assignment of the object reference. A reading thread can see a non-null reference to an object whose fields are still null or default.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class UnsafePublication {
    static Account account; // NOT volatile

    static void initialize() {
        // The JVM may execute in this order:
        // 1. Allocate memory for Account
        // 2. Assign reference to 'account' (now non-null!)
        // 3. Run constructor (set fields)
        // Another thread reads 'account' between step 2 and 3: fields are null!
        account = new Account("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("100000"));
    }
}

class Account {
    String id;
    BigDecimal balance;
    Account(String id, BigDecimal balance) {
        this.id = id;
        this.balance = balance;
    }
}
```

### How do final fields fix this?

The JMM guarantees that all final fields are visible after the constructor completes. This is the freeze semantic.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class SafeAccount {
    private final String id;             // final: guaranteed visible
    private final BigDecimal balance;     // final: guaranteed visible

    public SafeAccount(String id, BigDecimal balance) {
        this.id = id;
        this.balance = balance;
    }

    public String getId() { return id; }
    public BigDecimal getBalance() { return balance; }
}
// Any thread that obtains a reference to a SafeAccount after construction
// is guaranteed to see the final fields fully initialized.
```

Java records are ideal for safe publication because all record fields are implicitly final.

### How does a volatile reference fix this?

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class VolatilePublication {
    static volatile Account account; // volatile!

    static void initialize() {
        Account temp = new Account("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("100000"));
        account = temp; // volatile write: happens-before
        // Any thread reading 'account' after this volatile write
        // sees the fully constructed object.
    }
}
```

### How does double-checked locking require volatile?

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class ConnectionPool {
    private static volatile ConnectionPool instance; // MUST be volatile

    private ConnectionPool() {
        // expensive initialization
    }

    public static ConnectionPool getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {                    // first check (no lock)
            synchronized (ConnectionPool.class) {
                if (instance == null) {             // second check (with lock)
                    instance = new ConnectionPool(); // volatile write
                }
            }
        }
        return instance;
    }
}
// Without volatile: another thread may see non-null 'instance'
// with uninitialized fields (instruction reordering).
```

### How do thread-safe collections provide safe publication?

`ConcurrentHashMap.put()` has built-in happens-before guarantees.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;

public class CollectionPublication {
    static final ConcurrentHashMap<String, Account> cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    static void publish() {
        cache.put("SAV2001", new Account("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("100000")));
        // CHM.put() has happens-before: readers see fully constructed Account
    }
}
```

### What are the safe publication techniques summary?

| Technique | How | When to use |
|---|---|---|
| Final fields | JMM freeze semantic | Immutable objects, records |
| Volatile reference | Happens-before on volatile write | Mutable objects, singletons |
| Synchronized block | Monitor exit happens-before monitor enter | Complex initialization |
| Thread-safe collection | Built-in happens-before | Cache loading |
| Static initializer | Class loading is thread-safe | Eager singletons |

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Leaking this in constructor**: if the constructor passes `this` to another thread before construction completes, that thread may see a partially constructed object. Never leak `this` from a constructor.
- **Enum singletons**: `enum Singleton { INSTANCE; }` is safely published by the JVM's class loading mechanism. It is the simplest and safest singleton pattern.
- **Immutable objects are always safe**: an object with all final fields and no mutable state is safely published once any thread obtains a reference to it, even without volatile.
